index 7895f2f6b13bcfcb2e22a5eaca8b0c794445e90a..c3bb263460a472243616b3e066597d85f8fc6a68 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -212,7 +212,7 @@ In addition to the review workflow, the incur runtime also exposes:
 - `--wait` implies auto-send and uses a longer timeout budget: `10m` by default and `40m` in Deep Research mode.
 - When `--wait` is enabled, `review-gpt` stays attached until the assistant finishes or the wait timeout is hit. Deep Research runs can stay quiet for a long time before the final report arrives.
 - A response is only captured after it stays unchanged across consecutive quiet polls (no busy status, no stop control) for several seconds, so interim assistant status messages emitted before long tool/connector work are not mistaken for the final reply.
-- Waited runs that request a concrete model require an explicit `MODEL_CONFIRMATION` line. Matching confirmation passes normally; `UNKNOWN` is also accepted after at least 10 minutes of observed generation because reviewers cannot always inspect their own model identity. Missing confirmation and named model mismatches still fail closed. When ChatGPT model metadata is available, capture also validates it, including the UI-only GPT-5.6 Sol name's `gpt-5-6-pro` backend slug, and a known metadata mismatch always fails.
+- Waited runs that request a concrete model require exactly one unfenced, unquoted `MODEL_CONFIRMATION` line outside rendered quote and code containers in an assistant turn whose immediately preceding user turn is the exact turn committed by this run. An ephemeral per-run nonce at the start of the sent prompt disambiguates concurrent lanes and is used only for DOM turn binding; it is never written to attestation evidence. Concrete GPT targets also require a nonempty, matching ChatGPT response-model slug from that same assistant turn, including the UI-only GPT-5.6 Sol name's `gpt-5-6-pro` backend slug. A matching textual name or `UNKNOWN` is accepted only with that exact platform-owned response metadata; missing or multiple confirmation lines, pre-prompt or concurrently authored turns, absent or mismatched GPT metadata, and named model mismatches fail closed. A completed concrete-GPT capture emits a versioned `REVIEW_GPT_MODEL_VERIFICATION` JSON record with `requestedModel`, `responseModelSlug`, and `responseSha256`; the digest covers the exact normalized UTF-8 response-file bytes. With `--response-file`, the response and `<response-file>.model-verification.json` are written atomically with owner-only permissions before the success record is emitted. A new waited response-file attempt removes only that derived sidecar before browser work, and partial or failed captures never create attestation evidence.
 - `--response-marker <text>` makes capture contract-based instead of heuristic: the wait only accepts a response containing that exact text, so prompts that instruct the assistant to end its final message with the marker are immune to interim status messages even across minutes-long quiet gaps. If the marker never appears, capture fails after writing the best snapshot to `--response-file` when one was configured.
 - Browsers throttle background-tab rendering, which can freeze the DOM the capture reads mid-stream. The managed browser launches with backgrounding disabled and the capture session pins focus emulation and an active page lifecycle over CDP, so polling works with the tab fully in the background and never steals OS focus.
 - Deep Research auto-send gives the product up to 60 seconds to auto-start, then only falls back to the approval-card `Start` action if that gate is still present.
